---
import Pipeline from '../components/Pipeline.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { APP_ID, IMAGES, ORG_ID, REPO } from '../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'Every Pulsar image ships its full package list as an SPDX SBOM and is diffed package by package each night. The NVIDIA module and ISOs are signed; images aren’t yet.';

const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Every Pulsar image has a paper trail',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/provenance', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const SBOM = `oras discover ${IMAGES.vanilla}:latest`;
---

<Base title="Pulsar provenance — SBOMs and a nightly package diff" crumb="Provenance" description={DESCRIPTION} path="/provenance" jsonLd={JSONLD}>
  <main id="main" class="page">
    <arc-container size="lg">
      <arc-page-header border>
        <h1 slot="heading">Every image has a paper trail</h1>
        <p slot="description">The machine that runs Pulsar never compiles anything. A build machine that exists for one night builds every image and attaches its full package list, and the keys that matter never touch it.</p>
      </arc-page-header>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="one-night">
        {/* the timeline's steps are level-3 headings; this is their h2 */}
        <h2 class="sr-only" id="one-night">How one night's build runs</h2>
        <Pipeline />
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="verify">
        <h2>Don't take this page's word for it</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <p>
              Every published image carries its full package list as an SPDX SBOM, attached in the
              registry, so you can read it from whatever machine you already trust:{' '}
              <code>oras discover</code> lists it. The installer ISOs are signed with Pulsar’s cosign
              release key. The images themselves are not signed yet; signing them with that same key
              is coming, and this page will say so when it lands. On a Pulsar machine,{' '}
              <code>pulsar verify</code> tells you the same about the image you booted.
            </p>
            <p>
              An SPDX SBOM ships attached to each image, and every nightly is diffed against the one
              before it, package by package, from those SBOMs. The diff is rendered on the{' '}
              <a href="/changelog">changelog</a>, served raw as{' '}
              <a href="/changelog.json">changelog.json</a>, and readable on the machine as{' '}
              <code>pulsar changelog</code>. Nothing in it is written by hand.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="sbom" code={SBOM}></arc-code-block>
            <arc-description-list class="endpoints" layout="horizontal">
              <arc-description-item term="changelog.json"><a href="/changelog.json">the night's diff, as the build wrote it</a></arc-description-item>
              <arc-description-item term="manifest.json"><a href="/manifest.json">the image's baked manifest</a></arc-description-item>
              <arc-description-item term="cosign.pub"><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/keys/cosign.pub`}>the release key that signs the ISOs</a></arc-description-item>
            </arc-description-list>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="keys">
        <h2>Two variants, one key</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <p>
              The nvidia variant builds <code>nvidia-open</code> against the image's exact kernel and
              signs it. Blackwell has no closed-driver option, which is fine — the open module is the
              better one now anyway.
            </p>
            <p>
              <code>Containerfile</code> declares no build secrets, so it is safe to build anywhere.{' '}
              <code>Containerfile.nvidia</code> needs the Secure&nbsp;Boot signing key — which it
              never holds. The key lives on the signing host; the build carries a bearer token, sends
              each module's bytes, and attaches the detached signature that comes back. Neither key
              nor token reaches an image layer.
            </p>
            <p>
              The public half ships in <strong>both</strong> images so the key can be enrolled before
              the driver is in play. The nvidia build fails if the module's signer doesn't match that
              cert — a stale cert becomes a failed build instead of a black screen at boot.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>
              <strong>Running this yourself?</strong> Fork it and use your own key. Enrolling this
              cert means your machine permanently trusts modules its owner signs, which is not a
              relationship you want with a stranger's laptop. The standard image needs no keys at
              all.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>A build somebody starts by hand defines nothing.</strong> The scheduled
              nightly is the published one. A hand-started build is tagged <code>-dev</code> out of
              its own series: <code>:latest</code> does not move onto it, the SBOM baseline the next
              night diffs against is left alone, and no site commit lands. A <code>-dev</code> tag in
              the registry is a debugging image that was never released, and a machine booted on one
              says so in its boot menu.
            </p>
            <p class="hint">
              <span class="sent">One person's laptop OS, over-engineered on purpose, MIT-licensed.</span>{' '}
              <a href={REPO}>The whole thing is public.</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </arc-container>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .two {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    max-width: 72rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .two arc-code-block {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }
  .endpoints {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .two {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-md);
    }
  }
</style>
